  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhanced capacity of the ILO consitutents in promoting sustainable enterprises through better access to resources, higher productivity and improved workplace practices</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The transition to a green, low-carbon economy presents both unprecedented opportunities and significant risks for workers, businesses, and communities across various sectors. Without adequate planning and support, this transformation could lead to job displacement, widening inequalities, and economic disruption, particularly for vulnerable groups and small enterprises that form the backbone of Indonesia&apos;s economy. In biennium, the ILO is focusing on collaborating with government and private sector stakeholders to facilitate Indonesia&apos;s transition to a sustainable, inclusive, and green economy.  The rural economy plays a vital role in Indonesia&#x2019;s employment landscape, with over 40% of the population living in villages and depending on agriculture, fisheries, plantations, and micro and small enterprises. These sectors are major job providers and hold strong potential for inclusive and sustainable employment. However, structural challenges remain, including the dominance of the informal sector, low job quality, and limited access to training, finance, and markets. Social inclusion for women, youth, and vulnerable groups is also limited, requiring integrated solutions through skills development, infrastructure investment, and local economic empowerment. ILO focuses on the areas of coffee, patchouli, seaweed, and dairy cattle sectors, including introduces Enterprise Resources Platform (ERP) systems were introduced to strengthen cooperatives and financial access.</narrative>
  </description>
